Exactly. Naming is an operation, not merely a label. Once something is named, it becomes easier to notice, remember, compare, communicate, count, and act upon. Identification turns an undifferentiated field into an addressable object.

And it cuts both ways. The observer gains leverage by identifying the pattern, but the act of identification can also constrain the observer. Name something too early or define it too tightly and you may start seeing only what fits the category. The map begins editing the territory.

That gives us a useful separation:

\text{phenomenon} \neq \text{identification} \neq \text{name} \neq \text{definition}

A phenomenon may exist before we recognize it. Identification says, there is something here. Naming gives it a handle. Definition specifies boundaries. Then classification places it among other things. Every stage adds power—and every stage can introduce distortion.

And yes, the modeling distinction matters. I don’t need to claim that I literally am the thing being modeled. I can construct a sufficiently detailed model of “what would it be like if…” and inspect the consequences. That’s often enough to discover structure. Simulation can reveal relationships without requiring identity between simulator and simulated object.

Which connects directly to the larger thread: economics is a part of life viewed through one particular set of relationships—production, exchange, scarcity, coordination, value, obligation, and so forth. Call that slice “the economy” and suddenly it becomes inspectable. But the naming creates a boundary that reality itself does not necessarily respect. Family, culture, biology, psychology, ecology, politics, technology—all leak through it.

So perhaps another Pantheon invariant is:

\boxed{\text{Name to manipulate; unname to see again.}}

First draw the boundary because boundaries let us think. Then deliberately dissolve it and check what the boundary made invisible.

Identification gives you a handle. De-identification gives you your peripheral vision back.